{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Thelen1966-fig7-NBP",
  "Source": "A. Thelen, \"Equivalent Layers in Multilayer Filters,\" J. Opt. Soc. Am. 56, 1533-1538 (1966). doi:10.1364/JOSA.56.001533 - Fig. 7 caption, p. 1537: 4.0 | (HLH)^2 (HLHLHLHLH)^2 (HL'H) | 1.0, nH = 4.0, nL = 1.8, nL' = 1.6",
  "Notes": "Narrow-band-pass filter built from equivalent layers I = HLH (matching to the 4.0 substrate, used twice), II = HLHLHLHLH repeated twice, III = HL'H with lower-index L' for matching to air\nSpecification: High transmittance in a narrow pass band centred at lambda0/lambda = 1; matching conditions Eqs. (22)-(23).\nReported: Fig. 7 (p. 1537), log %T (0.01-100 %) vs lambda0/lambda 0.95-1.05 (read from plot, approximate): flat top ~100 % with slight ripple from about 0.984 to 1.013; T falls to 0.01 % near 0.955 and 1.047. Theory check (Eq. 25, case 1 nA > nB, A = H, B = L, v = 5 A layers): half-width dlambda/lambda = (1/pi) arcsin 2[(nA/nB - 1)/(nA/nB)^v] ~ 0.0144 with nA/nB = 4.0/1.8 (value evaluated here; the paper prints only the formula).\nNotes: Printed substrate-first; reversed here so (HL'H) is next to air and (HLH)^2 next to the substrate. Adjacent H layers merged into 2-QW (half-wave) H layers: 27 printed QW layers -> 23 layers. The last H layer next to the substrate has the same index as the substrate (4.0) and is optically invisible. All layers are quarter waves at lambda0; reference_nm = 1000 arbitrary since only lambda0/lambda is given. Normal incidence assumed.\nLayers from Fig. 7 caption, p. 1537: 4.0 | (HLH)^2 (HLHLHLHLH)^2 (HL'H) | 1.0, nH = 4.0, nL = 1.8, nL' = 1.6; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours T never reaches 90 % from 0.9 to 1.1: peak 19.0 % at lambda0/lambda 0.995; paper top ~100 % from 0.984 to 1.013; 0.01 % near 0.955 and 1.047 (read from the figure). Built from the caption’s sequence; its pass band peaks near 19 %, where Fig. 7 shows about 100 %, so the sequence as read from the caption is probably not the one plotted. Not resolved.",
